summary: MLS Enforcing-Mode Boot Validation (COPR-based validation of PR #3380, EXPERIMENTAL)
description: |
    EXPERIMENTAL sibling of tmt/mls-final.fmf (same directory). Validates
    upstream PR #3380
    (https://github.com/fedora-selinux/selinux-policy/pull/3380, "Enable
    cloud-init under MLS", 2 commits: the cloud-init fix + the udev_t
    MLS-boot-hang fix) as the REAL Packit/COPR-built selinux-policy-mls
    RPM package, instead of manually building/loading standalone .te
    modules on top of an unpatched package.

    Enables the Packit-managed COPR repo
    packit/fedora-selinux-selinux-policy-3380 (confirmed via the GitHub
    API against PR #3380 HEAD c3fbc760b9: rpm-build succeeded for
    fedora-44-x86_64, fedora-45-x86_64 and fedora-rawhide-x86_64) before
    installing selinux-policy-mls. Since dnf/dnf5 picks the highest EVR
    across ALL enabled repos (not just the newly-enabled one), and a
    fast-moving compose can already have a newer selinux-policy-mls build
    than Packit's PR snapshot (observed live on Fedora-Rawhide), the
    prepare script pins the exact NVR Packit built for PR #3380 on the
    fedora-44-x86_64 chroot -- so run this plan against --compose
    Fedora-44, not Fedora-Rawhide.

    This covers 6 of the 8 modules the production plan builds locally
    (cloudform + the 5 udev_t fixes now folded into udev.te upstream);
    mlssshpipes and udevptrace are NOT part of PR #3380 (not present
    anywhere in its diff) and are still built/loaded locally here.

    This is a one-off validation test, NOT a replacement for the
    production tmt/mls-final.fmf plan/mechanism. Do not treat a pass here
    as a reason to change mls-final.fmf without a separate, explicit
    decision to do so.

    Requires environment variable PR_NUMBER (the selinux-policy PR number
    whose Packit-managed COPR build, packit/fedora-selinux-selinux-policy-
    ${PR_NUMBER}, should be installed from). Defaults to 3380 below for
    manual/ad-hoc runs; a CI caller (e.g. `testing-farm request`) should
    override it with `-e PR_NUMBER=<n>` / `--tmt-environment PR_NUMBER=<n>`
    for whichever PR is actually under test.

    In addition to the boot/cloud-init/AVC check above, this plan also
    discovers and runs the real, canonical
    https://github.com/SELinuxProject/selinux-testsuite against this same
    MLS-enforcing, COPR-validated guest (see the second `discover` phase
    below) -- this repo's own tmt/plans.fmf `/ci` plan already wires up
    that same upstream testsuite via `how: fmf`, but only for a *separate*
    validation track: targeted policy + SELinux userspace rebuilt from
    this git checkout (STS_ROOT_DOMAIN: unconfined_t). That track is
    orthogonal to this plan (stock Fedora userspace + the COPR-built
    selinux-policy-mls RPM under test), so instead of reusing it as-is
    this plan adds its own `fmf` discover phase, without the
    /prepare-selinux-userspace build-from-git step (irrelevant here --
    we're validating the shipped RPM policy, not this checkout's
    userspace), and with STS_ROOT_DOMAIN set to sysadm_t instead of
    unconfined_t.

    sysadm_t (not unconfined_t) is the right root domain for MLS: this
    plan's own prepare script already turns on the `ssh_sysadm_login`
    boolean (needed for root's SSH session, which maps to
    sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t under MLS, to transition out of
    sshd_session_t at all -- see mls-final-copr-prepare.sh). MLS/strict
    policy has no unconfined_t. STS_ROOT_DOMAIN=sysadm_t is itself an
    upstream-supported, CI-tested value (selinux-testsuite's own
    .github/workflows/checks.yml matrix runs `domain: [unconfined_t,
    sysadm_t]`), and runtests.pl explicitly accepts running as root in
    sysadm_r (`$role ne "sysadm_r" && $role ne "unconfined_r" && $role ne
    "system_r"`). The testsuite is also directly MLS-aware, not just
    targeted/MCS-oriented: it auto-detects the running policy type via
    its own tests/pol_detect (NON-MLS/MCS/MLS, based on the kernel's
    initial SID level) and both tests/Makefile and policy/Makefile branch
    on that ($(POL_TYPE) = MLS/MCS) to build extra MLS/MCS-only test
    modules (test_glblub.cil for MLS, plus test_add_levels.cil for MCS)
    on top of the always-built test_mlsconstrain.cil -- i.e. it ships
    real, dedicated MLS test coverage, not something bolted on. The test
    policy itself is just semodule-loaded on top of whatever base policy
    is already active, so it does not assume/require targeted policy.

    The testsuite's own tmt/plans.fmf `/prepare-system` test (order: 1)
    only runs its STS_ROOT_DOMAIN/STS_KERNEL setup (and a possible
    tmt-reboot) when `$TMT_REBOOT_COUNT -eq 0`; since this plan's own
    prepare step already consumed 2 reboots switching to MLS+Enforcing
    before execute even starts, that setup is a no-op here by the time
    it runs -- harmless, since the sysadm_login boolean is already on
    from this plan's own prepare script and MLS's root login already
    maps to sysadm_u by default.

    Same filter as the existing `/ci` plan
    (`tag:-ci & tag:-nfs`): excludes the CI-only meta-tests (`/avc-check`,
    `/gitignore-coverage`) and the NFS subtest, which need infrastructure
    (a real nfs-server, git checkout cleanliness) irrelevant to this VM.

    RESULTS FROM A REAL RUN (Testing Farm request
    d1bebfbc-9770-4a68-a193-9dbe285ab1cd,
    https://artifacts.dev.testing-farm.io/d1bebfbc-9770-4a68-a193-9dbe285ab1cd):
    confirmed the whole mechanism above works end-to-end (COPR install,
    MLS switch, 2 reboots, our own boot/cloud-init/AVC check all PASS),
    and surfaced 2 real, MLS-specific problems in the upstream testsuite
    run itself -- neither is a problem with this plan's own prepare/COPR
    logic:

    - `/tmt/tests/run/main` (the actual selinux-testsuite regression
    suite) ERRORED before running a single real test case:
    `semodule -i test_policy/test_policy.pp ...` failed with
    `libsemanage.semanage_direct_install_file: Unable to read file
    test_policy/test_policy.pp. (Permission denied).` -- immediately
    after that same file was freshly built by the preceding `make -C
    policy load` step in the very same test/process. That first real run
    captured no ausearch/`ls -Z` evidence for it, so a follow-up
    diagnostic-only discover phase (`mls-testsuite-diag`, see below) was
    added and re-run (Testing Farm request
    a61990ea-0d68-4790-9891-0d45bc3a8f2f,
    https://artifacts.dev.testing-farm.io/a61990ea-0d68-4790-9891-0d45bc3a8f2f)
    specifically to capture hard evidence -- CONFIRMED, root cause now
    known with certainty via `ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC`:

    ```
    avc: denied { map } for pid=1760 comm=semodule
      path=.../tests/policy/test_policy/test_policy.pp
      scontext=sysadm_u:sysadm_r:semanage_t:s0-s15:c0.c1023
      tcontext=sysadm_u:object_r:var_t:s0 tclass=file permissive=0
    ```

    This IS a real, MLS-specific selinux-policy denial, not a DAC/tooling
    issue. The full deduplicated denial set from the diagnostic run's
    `ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC` output (Testing Farm request
    a61990ea-0d68-4790-9891-0d45bc3a8f2f, ~4300 raw AVC lines collapsed
    to these distinct patterns):

    ```
    { map }         sysadm_u:sysadm_r:checkpolicy_t  -> etc_t:file
    { map }         sysadm_u:sysadm_r:semanage_t     -> etc_t:file
    { map }         sysadm_u:sysadm_r:semanage_t     -> var_t:file   (fatal one)
    { relabelfrom } sysadm_u:sysadm_r:semanage_t     -> semanage_store_t:{dir,file}
                     (one repeat per pre-existing module file/dir in the
                     store -- hundreds of near-identical lines, same root cause)
    { noatsecure }  sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t       -> {checkpolicy_t,rpm_t,semanage_t}:process
    { noatsecure }  system_u:system_r:sshd_session_t -> sysadm_t:process
    { siginh }      (same 4 source/target pairs as noatsecure)
    { rlimitinh }   sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t       -> {checkpolicy_t,rpm_t}:process
    ```

    The `noatsecure`/`siginh`/`rlimitinh` denials are a red herring: they
    fire on *every* domain transition in this policy (including
    `sshd_session_t` -> `sysadm_t`, i.e. the SSH login itself, which
    plainly works), because `domain_transition_pattern()`
    (policy/support/misc_patterns.spt) has its
    `dontaudit $1 $3:process { noatsecure siginh rlimitinh }` line
    commented out -- these three permissions are ungated AT_SECURE/signal/
    rlimit-inheritance side effects of execve(), not something that
    blocks the transition itself. `rpm_t` shows the identical pattern
    with no functional impact (`dnf install` succeeds throughout this
    plan) -- confirming it never blocks anything.

    The real blocker is the `map`/`relabelfrom` set. Both `checkpolicy_t`
    and `semanage_t` were kept at sysadm_t's full clearance range
    (`s0-s15:c0.c1023`) as their own context -- but that is *not* a bug:
    reading base refpolicy's `policy/mls` constraints directly, `map` on
    file class has no `mlsconstrain` entry at all (so it is a plain
    type-enforcement permission, unaffected by MLS ranges), and the
    `relabelfrom` targets here are single-level (`s0`, no categories) so
    the "single level file write ops" constraint's `l1 eq l2` clause
    trivially holds (`s0 eq s0`) regardless of `checkpolicy_t`/
    `semanage_t`'s own range -- no `range_transition` would change either
    outcome. The actual gap: reading
    `policy/modules/system/selinuxutil.te`, both domains get essentially
    all of their real-world "read/relabel arbitrary admin content"
    access from `optional_policy(\`unconfined_domain($1)')` -- MLS ships
    no `unconfined` module at all, so that block never fires there,
    leaving both domains without `map` on generic content or
    `relabelfrom` on the module store when actually invoked (as
    intended) from the confined `sysadm_t` instead of targeted's
    unconfined root login.

    FIXED locally (this epic's scope: no selinux-policy PR/push, just a
    standalone module on top of the COPR build, same mechanism as
    `mlssshpipes`/`udevptrace`): `tmt/mls-semanage-access-fix/
    mlssemanageaccess.te` grants exactly the four missing rules above
    (`checkpolicy_t`/`semanage_t` `map` on `etc_t`, `semanage_t` `map` on
    `var_t`, `semanage_t` `relabelfrom` on `semanage_store_t` dir+file),
    built/loaded by `mls-final-copr-prepare.sh` alongside the other two
    non-upstreamed local fixes.

    CONFIRMED FIXED by re-running with this module (Testing Farm request
    bc9ce803-74e3-460f-a8d3-32a0e7e6157e,
    https://artifacts.dev.testing-farm.io/bc9ce803-74e3-460f-a8d3-32a0e7e6157e):
    `make -C policy load`'s `semodule -i test_policy/test_policy.pp ...`
    now succeeds with zero denials (no more "Permission denied"), and
    `run/main` goes on to actually execute the regression suite for the
    first time ever under this plan -- 41 subtests ran, 40 passed
    (`domain_trans`, `entrypoint`, `execshare`, `exectrace`, `mmap`,
    `unix_socket`, `ptrace`, `dyntrans`, `bounds`, ... -- the entire
    non-network suite). This confirms the map/relabelfrom fix above is
    both correct and sufficient for what it targets.

    Problem B (`inet_socket/tcp`, TF bc9ce803): subtest 41 failed with
    `connect: Connection refused` then `runcon: .../inet_socket/tcp/server:
    Permission denied` while `unix_socket` and 40 other subtests passed
    under the same confined `sysadm_t` session. Artifact
    `.../run/main-4/output.txt` shows the client-side
    `runcon -t test_inet_client_t` on the same `test_file_t` binaries
    succeeds; only `runcon -t test_inet_server_t` on the server binary
    fails. Upstream `policy/test_inet_socket.te` alone wraps
    `test_inet_server_t` (not the client) in `mcs_constrained()` when
    that macro exists -- it does on Fedora MLS policy headers even though
    the guest runs MLS, not MCS -- which breaks entering the server domain
    under MLS while the MCS-specific peer-label checks those macros exist
    to support are already covered separately by `test_mlsconstrain.cil`.
    This is not a COPR/PR #3380 gap and not a TF firewall issue (nothing
    is listening because the server never execs). Fixed in this plan by
    `/mls-patch-testsuite-inet` (order 0.5 in the
    `mls-boot-cloudinit` discover phase): patch the fmf-discovered
    testsuite tree to drop those two server-only ifdef blocks before the
    separate `selinux-testsuite` discover phase executes at all. TMT runs
    each discover phase's tests as a batch (see execute/internal.py:
    one enqueue per discover plugin), so `order: 2.5` in a later shell
    discover phase still ran *after* `/prepare-system` and `/run/main`
    had already built/loaded `test_policy.pp` from the unpatched
    `test_inet_socket.te` (TF 291f8d79). Order 0.5 in the first phase
    runs after discover has cloned the tree but before any testsuite test
    -- including `/prepare-system` (order 1), which does not build policy
    itself but precedes `/run/main` (order 3, `make -C .. test` →
    `make -C policy load`). The post-`run/main` SSH timeout on bc9ce803
    is treated as infra fallout from the long failing subtest, not the
    root cause.

    - `/tmt/tests/unprepare` FAILED, exactly as predicted below:
    `semanage login --modify -s unconfined_u root` errors under MLS
    (`libsemanage.dbase_llist_query: could not query record value` /
    `OSError: Error`) because `unconfined_u` is not a valid SELinux user
    on an MLS system. Root cause confirmed by reading upstream
    https://github.com/SELinuxProject/selinux-testsuite's own
    `tmt/tests.fmf` `/unprepare` test: its `STS_ROOT_DOMAIN = sysadm_t`
    branch unconditionally hardcodes `-s unconfined_u` when reverting
    root's login mapping, instead of reverting to whatever
    STS_ROOT_DOMAIN implies (`sysadm_u` here) -- an upstream oversight,
    not something wrong in our own prepare script (which already set
    root to `sysadm_u` correctly). No existing upstream issue/PR about
    this was found (GitHub search on SELinuxProject/selinux-testsuite for
    "unconfined_u"/"unprepare"/"STS_ROOT_DOMAIN" on 2026-09-10 found
    nothing relevant). Given this is third-party, upstream test code we
    don't control the merge timeline of, and the VM this runs against is
    a fully ephemeral, one-shot Testing Farm guest that does not need its
    SELinux login mappings to survive past this test session anyway,
    `/tmt/tests/unprepare` is excluded below (`exclude:`) rather than
    patched upstream or worked around with an extra post-unprepare fixup
    step -- it is a harmless, well-understood, non-blocking known issue,
    not a silent mask: this paragraph documents exactly why.

environment:
  PR_NUMBER: "3380"
  # See the description above: sysadm_t (not unconfined_t) is the correct
  # root domain for an MLS guest, and matches this plan's own prepare
  # script (ssh_sysadm_login boolean already on). STS_KERNEL is a no-op
  # here (see above) but set explicitly for documentation purposes.
  STS_ROOT_DOMAIN: sysadm_t
  STS_KERNEL: default

prepare:
  - name: Install MLS policy from PR #3380's COPR build, build/load the 2 remaining local fixes, switch to MLS and then Enforcing (2 reboots)
    how: shell
    script: $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-final-copr-prepare.sh

discover:
  - name: mls-boot-cloudinit
    how: shell
    tests:
      - name: /mls-boot-cloudinit-avc-check
        summary: Confirm cloud-init completed under cloud_init_t on the MLS-enforcing COPR guest; dump AVC denials informationally
        # Must run before the selinux-testsuite's own /prepare-system
        # (order: 1 below), which unconditionally truncates
        # /var/log/audit/audit.log -- running any later would erase the
        # boot-time AVC evidence this check depends on.
        order: 0
        duration: 10m
        test: $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-final-copr-execute.sh
      - name: /mls-patch-testsuite-inet
        summary: Drop mcs_constrained(test_inet_server_t) from discovered testsuite policy before testsuite execute phase (Problem B)
        # Same discover phase as the boot check so this runs before TMT
        # starts the selinux-testsuite discover phase's batch (where
        # /prepare-system and /run/main live). order 0.5 is after boot
        # (0) but before testsuite /prepare-system (1).
        order: 0.5
        duration: 2m
        test: $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-patch-testsuite-inet.sh
  - name: selinux-testsuite
    how: fmf
    url: https://github.com/SELinuxProject/selinux-testsuite
    ref: main
    filter: tag:-ci & tag:-nfs
    # Known, upstream, MLS-specific, non-blocking issue -- see the
    # "RESULTS FROM A REAL RUN" paragraph above for the full explanation.
    # /unprepare's STS_ROOT_DOMAIN=sysadm_t branch unconditionally tries
    # `semanage login --modify -s unconfined_u root`, which errors out
    # under MLS (unconfined_u does not exist there). This guest is
    # ephemeral and does not need that login mapping restored, so the
    # cleanest fix available to us (this is upstream, third-party test
    # code) is to just not run it, rather than let it fail loudly or mask
    # it silently.
    exclude:
      - /unprepare$
  - name: mls-testsuite-diag
    how: shell
    tests:
      - name: /mls-testsuite-diag-run-main-evidence
        summary: Capture real ausearch/ls-Z/id-Z evidence for the selinux-testsuite run/main "test_policy.pp Permission denied" failure (Problem A); purely informational, never blocks the plan
        # Must run after /selinux-testsuite/tmt/tests/run/main (order: 3,
        # where the failure happens) so there is something to capture,
        # and before /selinux-testsuite/tmt/tests/unprepare (order: 5,
        # harmless either way since it's excluded above) -- order: 4
        # lands exactly between them and does not collide with anything
        # else (the upstream /avc-check and /gitignore-coverage tests
        # that also use order: 4 are tag:[ci], already filtered out
        # above).
        order: 4
        duration: 5m
        test: $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-testsuite-diag.sh

execute:
  how: tmt
